<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::cross_border_attack_rate" target="ai_governance_militar::cyberattack_frequency" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Rises in cross‑border kinetic strikes tend to be accompanied by retaliatory or preemptive offensive cyber operations against command, control and critical systems (including AI infrastructure), increasing the observed frequency of successful cyberattacks.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">ai_governance_militar</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::cross_border_attack_rate" target="energy_supply_shock::fuel_price_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Repeated cross-border strikes on ships, ports and routes raise insurance and freight premiums and trigger route suspensions, creating an immediate market risk premium that pushes up refined fuel prices.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">energy_supply_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::cross_border_attack_rate" target="us_china_trade_tech::strategic_trust_level" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.55</data>
  <data key="d36">A rise in cross‑border strikes increases expectations on third parties to take clear positions or impose constraints; visible ambiguity or non‑cooperation from Beijing in the face of such incidents erodes U.S. perceptions of reliability and lowers bilateral strategic trust.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_trade_tech</data>
</edge>
